WEBVTT

00:00:00.288 --> 00:00:01.712
Today we’re looking at Erdős problem

00:00:01.712 --> 00:00:03.452
six forty-three, a question about when

00:00:03.452 --> 00:00:05.058
a pattern becomes unavoidable.

00:00:05.154 --> 00:00:06.413
We’ll explain the version with groups

00:00:06.413 --> 00:00:08.000
of three, using just six dots

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:08.837
to see what the problem is

00:00:08.837 --> 00:00:09.423
asking.

00:00:10.788 --> 00:00:12.125
Here is the three-element version of

00:00:12.125 --> 00:00:13.788
Erdős problem six forty-three.

00:00:13.904 --> 00:00:15.500
Start with six labeled dots.

00:00:15.587 --> 00:00:16.721
A triple is simply a group

00:00:16.721 --> 00:00:17.615
of three dots.

00:00:17.692 --> 00:00:18.779
We are going to collect triples

00:00:18.779 --> 00:00:20.173
and look for a particular pattern.

00:00:21.288 --> 00:00:22.731
Choose the triple one, two, three,

00:00:22.788 --> 00:00:24.385
and the triple four, five, six.

00:00:24.462 --> 00:00:25.462
They do not overlap.

00:00:25.567 --> 00:00:27.423
Together, they cover all six dots.

00:00:27.510 --> 00:00:28.356
This gives us one way to

00:00:28.356 --> 00:00:29.615
split these six dots into two

00:00:29.615 --> 00:00:30.173
triples.

00:00:31.308 --> 00:00:32.663
Now choose one, two, four, and

00:00:32.663 --> 00:00:33.923
three, five, six.

00:00:34.029 --> 00:00:35.558
These triples also do not overlap,

00:00:35.615 --> 00:00:36.894
and they cover exactly the same

00:00:36.894 --> 00:00:37.750
six dots.

00:00:37.856 --> 00:00:39.173
The four triples are distinct.

00:00:39.250 --> 00:00:40.769
If our collection contains all four,

00:00:40.846 --> 00:00:41.865
we have found the pattern.

00:00:42.978 --> 00:00:44.458
Possible groups do not count unless

00:00:44.458 --> 00:00:45.728
we actually selected them.

00:00:45.814 --> 00:00:47.824
Remove the triple three, five, six

00:00:47.824 --> 00:00:49.228
from this four-triple collection.

00:00:49.324 --> 00:00:50.881
The second split is incomplete.

00:00:50.978 --> 00:00:52.103
The pattern we just showed is

00:00:52.103 --> 00:00:53.054
no longer present.

00:00:54.125 --> 00:00:55.442
With more dots, we can choose

00:00:55.442 --> 00:00:56.529
many more triples.

00:00:56.606 --> 00:00:57.913
The question asks how large a

00:00:57.913 --> 00:00:59.644
collection must be before this repeated

00:00:59.644 --> 00:01:01.163
split becomes unavoidable.

00:01:01.298 --> 00:01:02.856
Finding one example is easy.

00:01:02.981 --> 00:01:04.798
Proving what every sufficiently large collection

00:01:04.798 --> 00:01:06.971
must contain is the mathematical challenge.
